WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.897
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.197 --> 00:00:08.282
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.582 --> 00:00:17.399
Unit two: Identity in a Globalised World. In this unit, you will discuss culture, belonging and identity in a connected world.

4
00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:19.953
cultural hybridity.

5
00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:24.986
cultural hybridity means the mixing of different cultures to create something new.

6
00:00:25.286 --> 00:00:28.702
Phnom Penh's food scene shows real cultural hybridity.

7
00:00:29.402 --> 00:00:32.960
Your turn. Say: cultural hybridity.

8
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.068
cultural hybridity.

9
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:39.235
assimilation.

10
00:00:39.935 --> 00:00:45.104
assimilation means the process of becoming part of a new culture, often losing your own.

11
00:00:45.404 --> 00:00:49.041
Full assimilation can mean losing your heritage language.

12
00:00:49.741 --> 00:00:52.498
Your turn. Say: assimilation.

13
00:00:55.198 --> 00:00:56.315
assimilation.

14
00:00:57.317 --> 00:00:58.480
diaspora.

15
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.431
diaspora means people who live outside the country their family came from.

16
00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:09.052
The Cambodian diaspora in California is large and active.

17
00:01:09.752 --> 00:01:12.405
Your turn. Say: diaspora.

18
00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:16.164
diaspora.

19
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.581
heritage language.

20
00:01:19.281 --> 00:01:25.262
heritage language means the language of your family, learned at home rather than at school.

21
00:01:25.562 --> 00:01:29.617
Many diaspora children speak Khmer only as a heritage language.

22
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:33.585
Your turn. Say: heritage language.

23
00:01:36.285 --> 00:01:37.715
heritage language.

24
00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:39.822
displacement.

25
00:01:40.522 --> 00:01:44.043
displacement means being forced to leave your home or country.

26
00:01:44.343 --> 00:01:47.445
War caused the displacement of millions of people.

27
00:01:48.145 --> 00:01:50.809
Your turn. Say: displacement.

28
00:01:53.509 --> 00:01:54.626
displacement.

29
00:01:55.617 --> 00:01:56.455
rootless.

30
00:01:57.155 --> 00:02:00.374
rootless means feeling that you don't belong anywhere.

31
00:02:00.674 --> 00:02:04.450
After moving six times, she felt completely rootless.

32
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.895
Your turn. Say: rootless.

33
00:02:10.595 --> 00:02:11.561
rootless.

34
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.062
global identity.

35
00:02:14.762 --> 00:02:20.488
global identity means a sense of belonging to the world rather than one country.

36
00:02:20.788 --> 00:02:25.725
Social media is helping young people develop a global identity.

37
00:02:26.425 --> 00:02:29.658
Your turn. Say: global identity.

38
00:02:32.358 --> 00:02:33.789
global identity.

39
00:02:34.783 --> 00:02:35.993
acculturation.

40
00:02:36.693 --> 00:02:41.015
acculturation means adapting to a new culture while keeping parts of your own.

41
00:02:41.315 --> 00:02:44.940
Acculturation is often healthier than complete assimilation.

42
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.652
Your turn. Say: acculturation.

43
00:02:51.352 --> 00:02:52.551
acculturation.

44
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.747
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:56.047 --> 00:03:00.104
My cousin grew up in Long Beach. She says she feels rootless.

46
00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:06.228
That's common in the diaspora. She's neither fully American nor fully Cambodian.

47
00:03:07.517 --> 00:03:11.978
She speaks Khmer only as a heritage language, mostly with her grandmother.

48
00:03:13.283 --> 00:03:20.057
Many families experience that. By next year, she'll have been living there for twenty-five years.

49
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.093
Do you think she should try to assimilate completely?

50
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:31.412
Researchers usually recommend acculturation instead: adapt, but keep your roots.

51
00:03:32.717 --> 00:03:37.038
I suppose cultural hybridity can be a strength rather than a loss.

52
00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:43.615
Absolutely. Many people now describe themselves as having a global identity.

53
00:03:44.917 --> 00:03:47.717
Grammar focus: Future perfect continuous.

54
00:03:48.017 --> 00:03:55.032
Use the future perfect continuous to stress how long an activity will have continued by a point in the future.

55
00:03:55.332 --> 00:03:59.491
Will have been plus -ing, often with by and for.

56
00:03:59.791 --> 00:04:05.483
For example: By 2030, I will have been living abroad for ten years.

57
00:04:05.783 --> 00:04:10.917
It emphasises how long an activity will have continued by a certain future time.

58
00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:14.755
Now read and listen. Between Two Worlds.

59
00:04:15.055 --> 00:04:21.350
Around the world, over two hundred and eighty million people live outside the country where they were born.

60
00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:28.771
For many of them, identity is not a single answer but a complex combination of places and languages.

61
00:04:30.083 --> 00:04:36.019
Children of migrants often grow up speaking a heritage language at home and another language at school.

62
00:04:37.317 --> 00:04:42.091
Some families choose assimilation, hoping their children will fit in more easily.

63
00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:48.936
Others prefer acculturation, encouraging children to adapt while keeping their traditions.

64
00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:55.036
Psychologists suggest that the second approach often leads to stronger self-esteem.

65
00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:03.377
Feeling rootless is common, yet many people eventually see cultural hybridity as a source of creativity.

66
00:05:04.683 --> 00:05:10.804
In a globalised world, belonging may depend less on geography and more on shared values.

67
00:05:12.117 --> 00:05:14.908
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:15.208 --> 00:05:19.611
People living outside their family's country of origin are the blank.

69
00:05:19.911 --> 00:05:24.377
A, diaspora. B, displacement. C, assimilation.

70
00:05:28.383 --> 00:05:31.138
The answer is A: diaspora.

71
00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.671
By December, I blank English for five years.

72
00:05:35.971 --> 00:05:41.296
A, will have been learning. B, will learn. C, have learned.

73
00:05:45.283 --> 00:05:48.072
The answer is A: will have been learning.

74
00:05:49.583 --> 00:05:52.012
How long blank there by the time you retire?

75
00:05:52.312 --> 00:05:57.127
A, will you work. B, will you have been working. C, have you worked.

76
00:06:01.117 --> 00:06:04.266
The answer is B: will you have been working.

77
00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:09.387
Adapting to a new culture while keeping your own is blank.

78
00:06:09.687 --> 00:06:15.430
A, assimilation. B, acculturation. C, rootless.

79
00:06:19.417 --> 00:06:22.055
The answer is B: acculturation.

80
00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:28.115
According to the text, which approach often leads to stronger self-esteem?

81
00:06:28.415 --> 00:06:34.193
A, assimilation. B, acculturation. C, isolation.

82
00:06:38.183 --> 00:06:40.810
The answer is B: acculturation.

83
00:06:42.317 --> 00:06:48.046
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is it possible to belong to more than one culture?

84
00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:58.445
Part two: Explain your view with two reasons and a personal example.

85
00:07:04.745 --> 00:07:18.046
I think it is. First, people can switch naturally between cultural codes; second, mixed identities often bring creativity. My cousin, for example, celebrates both Khmer New Year and Thanksgiving with equal pride.

86
00:07:18.346 --> 00:07:20.334
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:26.522
Great work today! You learned eight new words and future perfect continuous.

88
00:07:26.822 --> 00:07:31.425
Today's value is respect for culture. Be proud of our Khmer culture.

89
00:07:31.725 --> 00:07:34.133
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

90
00:07:34.433 --> 00:07:37.596
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

